uation: I'm in a hand of Texas Hold'em. The game tells me I have 19,900 chips left, so the big blind is 100. My hand is 5 of Spades and 2 of Clubs. The flop is Jack of Diamonds, 4 of Clubs, and 3 of Spades. This puts me with an open-ended straight draw; I'm holding 2, 3, 4, 5 and need an Ace or 6 to complete a straight. My opponent, who was the small blind and called pre-flop, checked the flop.\n\nLet's break it down: pre-flop I was the big blind and checked after the small blind called. Now, the pot is only 200, representing the combined blinds. Since my opponent checked to me, I need to evaluate the most optimal strategy. My hand is currently weak, just a 5-high, but the open-ended straight draw is strong.\n\nHere's my thought process: Betting might fold out some marginally better hands, but the pot is so small that a semi-bluff seems ill-advised. Folding is completely out of the question with the potential I have. All-in is ridiculous.\n\nGiven that my opponent checked, the best play is to check back and see the turn card for free. This allows me to see if I hit my straight without risking any more chips. It's a low-risk, high-potential move. Therefore, I will check.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: You hold 5 of Spades and 2 of Clubs on a board of Jack of Diamonds, 4 of Clubs, and 3 of Spades. This gives you an Open-Ended Straight Draw (2-3-4-5), meaning any Ace or 6 will complete a straight. Currently, you have only 5-high, which has no showdown value. The opponent has checked to you.\n\nReason: Since you have a strong drawing hand (8 outs to a straight) but currently hold very weak value (5-high), the best play is to Check (0). This allows you to see the Turn card for free. There is no need to bet and risk being raised when you can realize your equity without cost.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
