sk and reward. With the current situation, I need to fold and preserve my chips.\n\nLooking at the options, if I want to play, I need to match the bet of 100 or raise 200, according to the interface. Considering the hand I am holding, it is likely that, as an expert in the field, it is the best decision to fold, since it\'s most cost-effective.\n\n\n', 'output': "Evaluation: The current board is King of Diamonds, 9 of Diamonds, 6 of Clubs, 4 of Clubs. My hand is 7 of Clubs and Queen of Hearts, giving me Queen High. I have no flush draw (only 3 clubs) and no straight draw (I need two specific running cards, but only the River is left). The opponent checked the flop and bet 100 on the Turn. This bet likely indicates a made hand (like a pair of Kings, 9s, or 6s) or a semi-bluff. To win, I would need to hit a Queen or 7 on the river (approx. 13% chance) and hope it's the best hand, which is not guaranteed. The pot odds do not justify calling with such low equity. Bluffing via a raise is risky as the opponent's small bet often signifies a willingness to call or a 'milking' bet with a strong hand. Preserving chips is the prudent play.\n\nReason: I have a very weak hand (Queen High) with no potential for a straight or flush on the river. The opponent has shown aggression on the Turn. The probability of improving to a winning hand is very low, making a Call negative expected value. Folding is the safest option to preserve my chip stack.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
